(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-05"] = {"chem-12-05-m01":{"idea":"Metals and electrolytes both carry current, but the charge carriers are different.","steps":["In a metal, free electrons carry the current. The metal itself does not change.","In an electrolyte solution, ions carry the current.","At the electrodes, oxidation and reduction reactions take place.","So metals pass electrons with no chemical change, and solutions pass ions with reactions at the electrodes."],"others":"Swapping ions and electrons is wrong. Metallic conduction needs no chemical change. Electrons do not travel through the solution; ions do.","answer":"metals pass electrons with no chemical change; solutions pass ions with reactions at the electrodes"},"chem-12-05-m02":{"idea":"The names anode and cathode are defined by the reaction, not by the sign.","steps":["Oxidation means loss of electrons.","The electrode where oxidation happens is called the anode.","Reduction happens at the cathode.","This holds for galvanic and electrolytic cells alike."],"others":"Reduction takes place at the cathode. The electrolyte and the salt bridge are not electrodes.","trap":"Remember: An Ox, Red Cat.","answer":"the anode"},"chem-12-05-m03":{"idea":"In a galvanic cell the sign of each electrode comes from its reaction.","steps":["Oxidation at the anode releases electrons onto it.","So the anode is the negative terminal.","Reduction at the cathode uses electrons, so it is the positive terminal.","So the order is negative, then positive."],"others":"Both negative or both positive cannot be right, as the two terminals have opposite signs. Positive anode and negative cathode is the polarity in an electrolytic cell.","trap":"In an electrolytic cell the signs are reversed.","answer":"negative and positive"},"chem-12-05-m04":{"idea":"The salt bridge keeps the cell working by letting ions pass.","steps":["The anode half-cell gains positive ions as the metal dissolves.","The cathode half-cell loses positive ions as metal deposits.","Ions flow through the salt bridge to balance the charges.","This completes the circuit and keeps both half-cells neutral."],"others":"It does not stop electrodes dissolving. It does not raise the voltage. Electrons come from the anode and travel through the wire, not through the bridge.","answer":"complete the circuit by letting ions move, keeping both half-cells neutral"},"chem-12-05-m05":{"idea":"The SHE is the reference electrode, set to zero by agreement.","steps":["Only a cell voltage can be measured, so we need a reference.","The SHE is platinum in 1 M H⁺ with H₂ gas at 1 bar.","Its potential is taken as zero at all temperatures."],"others":"Oxygen with OH⁻, zinc with Zn²⁺ and silver with Ag⁺ are not the standard reference and do not have zero potential.","answer":"a platinum electrode in 1 M H⁺ with hydrogen gas at 1 bar, and its potential is taken as zero"},"chem-12-05-m06":{"idea":"EMF is the difference of the two electrode potentials.","steps":["Use reduction potentials for both electrodes.","The electrode with the higher potential is the cathode.","E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.","A positive value means the reaction is spontaneous."],"others":"Reversing the order gives the wrong sign. A product or sum of potentials has no meaning for a cell voltage.","trap":"Cathode minus anode.","answer":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode"},"chem-12-05-m07":{"idea":"The electrode with the lower reduction potential is oxidised, so it is the anode.","steps":["E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V and E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V.","Zinc is lower, so it is oxidised: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. It is the anode.","Copper is higher, so Cu²⁺ is reduced. Copper is the cathode.","EMF = 0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V."],"others":"The other pairs have the same metal at both ends, or have zinc as the cathode, which does not fit the potentials.","answer":"copper and zinc"},"chem-12-05-m08":{"idea":"A reaction is spontaneous when E°cell is positive.","steps":["The metal with the lower E° is oxidised and displaces the other.","Zn (−0.76 V) is lower than Cu (+0.34 V).","So Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu has E°cell = +1.10 V.","The reverse reactions have negative E°cell."],"others":"Cu cannot displace Zn²⁺, Ag cannot displace Cu²⁺, and Ag cannot displace Zn²⁺, because each would have a negative E°cell.","answer":"Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu"},"chem-12-05-m09":{"idea":"A large positive E° means the species gains electrons very easily.","steps":["E° is a reduction potential.","The larger it is, the more easily the species is reduced.","A species that is reduced easily takes electrons from others.","So it is the strongest oxidising agent."],"others":"The strongest reducing agent has the most negative E°. The most reactive metal is also at the negative end. The species oxidised most easily also has a very negative E°.","answer":"the strongest oxidising agent"},"chem-12-05-m10":{"idea":"The Nernst equation shows how E changes with concentration.","steps":["At 298 K: E = E° − (0.0591 / n) log Q.","n is in the denominator, since more electrons transferred make E less sensitive.","As the reaction proceeds, Q increases and E decreases.","At equilibrium E = 0."],"others":"Multiplying 0.0591 by n, inverting it, or using a plus sign are all wrong. A plus sign would make E grow as Q grows.","answer":"E = E° − (0.0591/n) log Q"},"chem-12-05-m11":{"idea":"The electrical work of a cell equals the fall in Gibbs energy.","steps":["Maximum electrical work = nFE°.","This work equals the decrease in free energy.","So ΔG° = −nFE°.","A positive E° gives a negative ΔG°."],"others":"The forms that divide by E° or n have the wrong units. ΔG° = nFE° misses the minus sign, which would make a positive E° go with a positive ΔG°.","answer":"ΔG° = −nFE°"},"chem-12-05-m12":{"idea":"A dead cell has reached equilibrium.","steps":["At equilibrium Q = K and ΔG = 0.","Since ΔG = −nFE, E = 0.","The cell can do no more work."],"others":"A dead cell does not keep its standard EMF. A negative EMF would mean the reverse reaction is spontaneous, which is not the case at equilibrium. The maximum EMF is at the start.","answer":"zero, and the cell reaction is at equilibrium"},"chem-12-05-m13":{"idea":"Faraday's first law links the mass deposited to the charge passed.","steps":["m = Z × Q, and Q = I × t.","Z is the electrochemical equivalent.","So mass is proportional to the charge.","Double the charge, double the mass."],"others":"Mass is not related to the square of the current. It does not depend on the volume of the electrolyte. It does not depend on temperature in this law.","answer":"the quantity of charge passed through the electrolyte"},"chem-12-05-m14":{"idea":"Faraday's second law compares different substances for the same charge.","steps":["The same charge carries the same number of electrons.","These deposit the same number of equivalents of each substance.","So the masses are in the ratio of their equivalent masses.","Equivalent mass = molar mass / electrons per ion."],"others":"Densities, atomic numbers and melting points do not decide how much is deposited.","answer":"their equivalent masses"},"chem-12-05-m15":{"idea":"One faraday is the charge on one mole of electrons.","steps":["F = N_A × e.","F = 6.022 × 10²³ × 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 96 485 C/mol.","We use 96 500 C in calculations."],"others":"6.02 × 10²³ is the Avogadro number, not a charge. 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C is the charge of one electron. 9650 C is ten times too small.","answer":"96 500 C"},"chem-12-05-m16":{"idea":"Work out the unit from the formula for molar conductivity.","steps":["Λ_m = κ × 1000 / C.","κ is in S/cm and C is in mol/L, so 1000/C is in cm³/mol.","S/cm × cm³/mol = S cm² mol⁻¹."],"others":"Ohm is the unit of resistance, not conductance. S cm⁻¹ is the unit of conductivity κ. S mol cm⁻² is not what the formula gives.","answer":"S cm² mol⁻¹"},"chem-12-05-m17":{"idea":"Molar conductivity is conductivity per mole of dissolved electrolyte.","steps":["On dilution, ions are farther apart and interact less.","A weak electrolyte also ionises more.","Conductivity κ falls, since fewer ions per volume, but Λ_m rises.","So Λ_m increases on dilution."],"others":"It does not decrease, because Λ_m is per mole, not per litre. It does not stay the same. It does not fall to zero.","answer":"increases, as the ions become freer and a weak electrolyte ionises more"},"chem-12-05-m18":{"idea":"Kohlrausch's law says that each ion contributes independently.","steps":["At infinite dilution the ions do not affect each other.","So Λ°_m = ν₊λ°₊ + ν₋λ°₋.","This is a sum, not a difference or product.","It is used to find Λ° for weak electrolytes."],"others":"A difference or product of ion conductivities has no basis. The conductivity of pure solvent is not the sum of ion contributions.","answer":"the sum of the molar conductivities of its cation and its anion"},"chem-12-05-m19":{"idea":"In a molten salt, only its own ions can be discharged.","steps":["Molten NaCl has Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions only.","Cathode (reduction): Na⁺ + e⁻ → Na.","Anode (oxidation): 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻.","So sodium forms at the cathode and chlorine at the anode."],"others":"Chlorine at the cathode and sodium at the anode swaps the electrodes. There is no oxygen, as there is no water. Hydrogen forms only if water is present.","answer":"sodium metal and chlorine gas"},"chem-12-05-m20":{"idea":"With copper electrodes, the anode itself is oxidised.","steps":["Cu is oxidised more easily than water or sulphate.","Anode: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻, so copper dissolves.","Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so copper deposits.","This is how copper is refined."],"others":"Oxygen does not form here. Hydrogen does not form, and copper does not dissolve at the cathode. Copper cannot deposit at the anode.","answer":"copper dissolves at the anode and deposits at the cathode"},"chem-12-05-m21":{"idea":"Know the parts of a dry cell.","steps":["The zinc container is oxidised: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻.","So zinc is the anode.","A graphite rod with MnO₂ is the cathode.","NH₄Cl and ZnCl₂ paste is the electrolyte."],"others":"Graphite is the cathode rod. MnO₂ is a depolariser around the graphite. Ammonium chloride is the electrolyte.","answer":"zinc"},"chem-12-05-m22":{"idea":"Follow what happens to the acid in the discharge reaction.","steps":["Pb + PbO₂ + 2H₂SO₄ → 2PbSO₄ + 2H₂O.","Sulphuric acid is used up.","Water is made.","So the solution becomes less dense."],"others":"The acid is a reactant, not a catalyst, and water is made, not used up. PbSO₄ is insoluble and sticks on the plates.","answer":"decreases, since sulphuric acid is used up and water forms"},"chem-12-05-m23":{"idea":"Fuel cells get their reactants from outside.","steps":["H₂ and O₂ are fed continuously to the electrodes.","The product is water.","It works as long as fuel is supplied.","A dry cell holds a fixed amount of reactants."],"others":"A fuel cell does not store reactants inside, and it does have electrodes. It is not a single-use cell. The product is water, not a salt or hydrogen peroxide.","answer":"the reactants are supplied continuously from outside, and the product is water"},"chem-12-05-m24":{"idea":"Rusting is a small electrochemical cell on the iron surface.","steps":["Moisture forms tiny cells on the iron.","At the anodic region iron is oxidised: Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻.","At the cathodic region oxygen is reduced.","Fe²⁺ is further oxidised to rust, Fe₂O₃·xH₂O."],"others":"Iron sulphide is not rust. Iron is oxidised, not reduced, at the anode. Rusting needs moisture, so dry air alone does not rust iron quickly.","answer":"oxidised at the anodic regions, and oxygen is reduced at the cathodic regions in the presence of moisture"},"chem-12-05-m25":{"idea":"Zinc protects iron by sacrifice.","steps":["E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V is lower than E°(Fe²⁺/Fe) = −0.44 V.","Zinc is oxidised more easily than iron.","At a scratch, zinc becomes the anode and corrodes.","Iron stays as the cathode, so it does not rust."],"others":"Zinc does not alloy at the scratch. Zinc is more, not less, easily oxidised. Zinc does react with water and air to a slight extent.","answer":"zinc is more easily oxidised than iron, so it corrodes in place of the iron"},"chem-12-05-m26":{"idea":"The sign of E°cell tells whether the reaction goes by itself.","steps":["ΔG° = −nFE°.","If E°cell is positive, ΔG° is negative.","A negative ΔG° means a spontaneous reaction.","The cell can deliver current."],"others":"Equilibrium would be E = 0. A cell with positive E° can deliver current. Non-spontaneous would need negative E°.","answer":"the cell reaction is spontaneous as written"},"chem-12-05-m27":{"idea":"Direct current would cause electrolysis and change the solution.","steps":["With direct current, ions are discharged at the electrodes.","This changes the composition of the solution near them.","Alternating current reverses direction quickly.","So there is no net electrolysis and the reading is stable."],"others":"Heating is not the main reason. Increasing the cell voltage is not the aim. With alternating current, the ions do not all move in one direction.","answer":"no net electrolysis takes place at the electrodes"},"chem-12-05-n01":{"idea":"Faraday's law: find charge, then moles of electrons, then moles of copper.","steps":["Q = I × t = 2.0 × (30 × 60) = 3600 C.","Moles of electrons = 3600 / 96500 = 0.0373 mol.","Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so moles of Cu = 0.0373 / 2 = 0.01865.","Mass = 0.01865 × 63.5 = 1.18 g."],"trap":"Change minutes to seconds. Two electrons per copper ion.","answer":"1.18 g"},"chem-12-05-n02":{"idea":"Find the charge needed for 10.8 g of silver, then the time.","steps":["Moles of Ag = 10.8 / 108 = 0.1 mol.","Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag, so 0.1 mol of electrons is needed.","Q = 0.1 × 96500 = 9650 C.","t = Q / I = 9650 / 5.0 = 1930 s."],"trap":"One electron per silver ion.","answer":"1930 s"},"chem-12-05-n03":{"idea":"Use E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.","steps":["Copper has the higher potential, so it is the cathode: +0.34 V.","Zinc is the anode: −0.76 V.","E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76).","E°cell = 1.10 V."],"trap":"The EMF of a working cell must be positive.","answer":"1.10 V"},"chem-12-05-n04":{"idea":"Use ΔG° = −nFE°.","steps":["n = 2, F = 96500 C/mol, E° = 1.10 V.","ΔG° = −2 × 96500 × 1.10.","ΔG° = −212 300 J.","In kJ: −212.3 kJ."],"trap":"A positive E° must give a negative ΔG°.","answer":"-212.3 kJ"},"chem-12-05-n05":{"idea":"Use the Nernst equation for the half-cell Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu.","steps":["E = E° − (0.0591 / 2) log(1 / [Cu²⁺]).","[Cu²⁺] = 0.01, so 1 / [Cu²⁺] = 100 and log 100 = 2.","E = 0.34 − 0.02955 × 2.","E = 0.281 V."],"trap":"A more dilute solution lowers the potential below E°.","answer":"0.281 V"},"chem-12-05-n06":{"idea":"Use Λ_m = κ × 1000 / C.","steps":["κ = 1.5 × 10⁻³ S/cm and C = 0.1 mol/L.","Λ_m = 1.5 × 10⁻³ × 1000 / 0.1.","Λ_m = 1.5 / 0.1 = 15.0 S cm²/mol."],"trap":"Use κ in S/cm and C in mol/L with the factor 1000.","answer":"15.0 S cm²/mol"},"chem-12-05-n07":{"idea":"Use Kohlrausch's law to combine known values.","steps":["CH₃COOH = CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺.","Take CH₃COONa and HCl, and subtract NaCl to cancel Na⁺ and Cl⁻.","Λ°(CH₃COOH) = Λ°(HCl) + Λ°(CH₃COONa) − Λ°(NaCl).","= 426 + 91 − 126 = 391 S cm²/mol."],"trap":"Na⁺ and Cl⁻ are added and removed, so they cancel.","answer":"391 S cm²/mol"},"chem-12-05-n08":{"idea":"Use the formula given for log K.","steps":["log K = nE° / 0.0591.","n = 2 and E° = 1.10 V.","log K = 2 × 1.10 / 0.0591 = 2.20 / 0.0591.","log K = 37.23."],"trap":"K is about 10³⁷, so the reaction goes almost fully to products.","answer":"37.23"},"chem-12-05-n09":{"idea":"Charge divided by F gives moles of electrons.","steps":["Q = I × t = 0.5 × 3600 = 1800 C.","Moles of electrons = 1800 / 96500 = 0.01865 mol.","Convert to millimoles: × 1000 = 18.65 mmol."],"trap":"One hour is 3600 s.","answer":"18.65 mmol"},"chem-12-05-n10":{"idea":"Find moles of electrons, then moles of H₂, then volume at STP.","steps":["Moles of electrons = 1930 / 96500 = 0.02 mol.","2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂, so moles of H₂ = 0.02 / 2 = 0.01 mol.","V = 0.01 × 22.4 L = 0.224 L.","V = 224 mL."],"trap":"Two electrons give one H₂ molecule.","answer":"224 mL"}};
